answer(1,p('During a fall, gravitational potential energy becomes kinetic energy. During impact the ball briefly stores elastic potential energy as it deforms. The rebound converts some stored energy back into kinetic and gravitational potential energy. Sound and thermal energy also result, so the rebound is usually lower. Energy is transferred between forms; it is not destroyed.'))
answer(2,p('Convert 46 g to 0.046 kg. For a steady lift, F = mg = (0.046)(9.81) = 0.451 N.','W = Fh = (0.046)(9.81)(0.95) = 0.4287 J ≈ <strong>0.43 J</strong>. This is the golfer’s positive work against gravity, assuming negligible change in speed.'))
answer(3,p('The force acts along the displacement: W = Fd = (25)(4.3) = 107.5 J ≈ <strong>1.1 × 10² J</strong>.'))
answer(4,p('Use vertical rise, rather than distance along the stairs: W = mgh = (70.0)(9.81)(25.0) = <strong>1.72 × 10⁴ J</strong> of useful work against gravity.'))
answer(5,p('The displacement is zero, so W = Fd = (300.0)(0) = <strong>0 J</strong> of mechanical work on the car. The person still expends chemical energy; the 10.0 s does not make the mechanical work nonzero.'))
answer(6,p('P = W/Δt = 4200/70.0 = <strong>60 W</strong>. This is an average output of 60 J each second.'))
answer(7,p('Total lifting work = 42mgh = (42)(24)(9.81)(1.6) = 15 821.6 J. The time is 3.6 × 60 = 216 s.','P = 15 821.6/216 ≈ <strong>73 W</strong> of useful average lifting power.'))
answer(8,p('Δt = 1.0 h = 3600 s. E = PΔt = (150)(3600) = <strong>5.4 × 10⁵ J</strong>, transferred into light and thermal energy. This is an energy amount, rather than J/h.'))
answer(9,parts('Input energy: E<sub>in</sub> = (1.5 × 10⁸)(2.0 × 3600) = 1.08 × 10¹² J. Useful work = 0.25E<sub>in</sub> = <strong>2.7 × 10¹¹ J</strong>.','Other output = 0.75E<sub>in</sub> = <strong>8.1 × 10¹¹ J</strong>, mostly thermal energy in the model. Total energy is conserved.'))
answer(10,p('Work = (650)(12) = 7800 J. P = 7800/11 = 709 W ≈ <strong>7.1 × 10² W</strong>.','With 1 hp = 746 W, power = 709/746 ≈ <strong>0.95 hp</strong>. The sample’s rounded 750 W/hp gives the same two-significant-figure result.'))
answer(11,p('Compare your measured W<sub>in</sub> = F<sub>effort</sub>d<sub>effort</sub> with W<sub>out</sub> = (2.0)(0.100) = 0.20 J. A real apparatus normally requires <strong>more input work than useful output work</strong>; an ideal apparatus requires equal work.','Use your actual measurements and uncertainties. A small apparent reversal can indicate measurement error rather than energy creation.'))
answer(12,p('A pulley can reduce effort force or change the pull direction, but the smaller force acts over a longer distance. It does <strong>not save ideal work</strong>: input and output work are equal. Friction makes real input work larger. Pulleys make large loads manageable.'))
answer(13,p('For each measured system, <strong>actual MA = load force / effort force = 2.0 N / your measured effort force</strong>. No effort readings are supplied, so measured numerical MA values cannot be filled in.','For comparison, ideal predictions for the pictured arrangements are:')+table(['Arrangement','Ideal MA'],[('Figure 5.1.1','2'),('Figure 5.1.2 A','2'),('Figure 5.1.2 B','1'),('Figure 5.1.2 C','3'),('Figure 5.1.2 D','4')]))
answer(14,p('Count the equal-tension rope segments supporting the moving load assembly, including an anchored rope end if it is attached to that assembly. For the simple systems pictured, their number gives ideal MA: <strong>2, 2, 1, 3, and 4</strong> in the order Figure 5.1.1, A, B, C, D.','Actual MA is commonly lower because of friction and moving pulley weight. Compare these predictions with your measured force ratios.'))
answer(15,p('Conservation of work for an ideal machine gives F<sub>e</sub>d<sub>e</sub> = F<sub>L</sub>d<sub>L</sub>. Thus <strong>IMA = d<sub>e</sub>/d<sub>L</sub></strong>. An effort moving four times as far as the load gives ideal MA = 4.'))
answer(16,p('This is a cascade of <strong>three movable-pulley stages</strong>, each ideally doubling force. The advantages multiply: IMA = 2 × 2 × 2 = <strong>8</strong>.','The effort moves eight times the load distance. Counting all visible rope segments as if they shared one tension would miss the separate stages. Test the prediction with the actual apparatus.'))
answer(17,p('Reverse a movable-pulley arrangement: apply the effort to its axle, keep one rope end fixed below, and attach the load to the other end. Ideally, the effort is twice the load and the load moves twice the effort distance, so <strong>MA = ½</strong>.','This trades greater force for greater load speed or travel; it is useful when fast or long movement matters more than reducing effort.')+reverse_pulley())
answer(18,p('<strong>Your measurements are required.</strong> For every trial calculate P<sub>out</sub> = mgh/Δt, then report the largest measured value and its conditions. With a 0.0500 kg mass, P = 0.4905h/Δt in watts when h is in metres and time in seconds.','The suggested 2–3 s is a target lifting time, not a complete set of data. Height and actual time are still needed.'))
answer(19,p('Collect the classmates’ measured maximum output powers, all calculated from mgh/Δt, and identify the largest. <strong>No class measurements are provided</strong>, so there is no predetermined numerical maximum. Check that units, loads, and timing definitions are comparable.'))
answer(20,p('Gravity acts along the downward displacement: W<sub>g</sub> = mgd = (10.0)(9.81)(5.0) = 490.5 J ≈ <strong>+4.9 × 10² J</strong>. The work by gravity is positive.'))
answer(21,p('Ignoring friction and any change in speed, the girl’s useful work against gravity is mgh = (110)(9.81)(1.2) = 1294.9 J ≈ <strong>1.3 × 10³ J</strong>.','Use the vertical rise 1.2 m. The 3.0 m ramp reduces effort force while increasing effort distance. Strictly, net work on the bike is zero if its speed is unchanged; the question intends the positive work supplied by the girl.'))
answer(22,parts('Lifting at steady speed: W = Fh = (98.0)(1.2) = 117.6 J ≈ <strong>1.2 × 10² J</strong>.','During a level, steady horizontal carry, the upward support force is perpendicular to displacement, so its work is <strong>0 J</strong>. Starting, stopping, or lifting the box would involve additional transfers.'))
answer(23,p('W = mgh = (25)(9.81)(350) = 85 837.5 J ≈ <strong>8.6 × 10⁴ J</strong> of useful work on the load.'))
answer(24,p('For a horizontal push, N = 100.0 N and friction = μN = (0.250)(100.0) = 25.0 N. At steady speed the push balances friction.','Work by the push = (25.0)(15.0) = <strong>375 J</strong>. Friction does −375 J; net work on the box is zero.'))
answer(25,parts('Effort distance increases by 8/2 = <strong>4 times</strong>, so it does not improve in the sense of requiring less movement.','Effort force falls to <strong>one quarter</strong> of the former value; this is the main advantage.','Ideal work done by you is <strong>unchanged</strong>, for the same load and height.','Power depends on time. For the same lifting time it is unchanged; at the same effort pulling speed, the load rises more slowly and useful power is lower. MA alone does not determine power.','Work done on the load by the machine is <strong>unchanged</strong>, equal to its weight times the same height.'))
answer(26,ANSWERS['q-17'])
answer(27,p('P = mgh/Δt = (500.0)(9.81)(12.0)/12 = 4905 W ≈ <strong>4.9 × 10³ W</strong> or 4.9 kW.'))
answer(28,parts('P = W/Δt = (25 × 10⁶)/3600 = 6944 W ≈ <strong>6.9 kW</strong>.','Use the specified 750 W/hp: 6944/750 = <strong>9.3 hp</strong> after rounding.'))
answer(29,p('E = PΔt = (100.0)(12.0 × 3600) = <strong>4.32 × 10⁶ J</strong> = 4.32 MJ.'))
answer(30,p('η = P<sub>useful</sub>/P<sub>in</sub> = 1200/1500 = 0.80, so the efficiency is <strong>80%</strong>.'))

answer(31,p('The sand may warm because shaking does mechanical work on the sand and container. Collisions and friction transfer some of this work into microscopic internal energy.','Record the actual temperatures; a detectable rise is not guaranteed for every container and thermometer because energy also spreads to the container and room.'))
answer(32,p('E<sub>k</sub> = ½mv². Doubling mass doubles kinetic energy, while doubling speed gives four times the kinetic energy. <strong>Doubling clubhead speed has the greater effect</strong> in this comparison; energy delivered to the ball also depends on the collision.'))
answer(33,p('Total mass = 110 + 60.0 = 170 kg; final speed = 80/3.6 = 22.22… m/s.','Net work = ΔE<sub>k</sub> = ½(170)(22.22…)² = 41 975 J ≈ <strong>4.2 × 10⁴ J</strong>. Extra engine work may also be needed to overcome resistance.'))
answer(34,p('v<sub>i</sub> = 80/3.6 m/s and v<sub>f</sub> = 50/3.6 m/s. W<sub>net</sub> = ½(1200)[(50/3.6)² − (80/3.6)²] = <strong>−1.81 × 10⁵ J</strong> before rounding.','About 1.8 × 10⁵ J of kinetic energy is removed. Braking and resistive forces do the negative work, transferring energy mainly into thermal energy.'))
answer(35,p('m = 1.0 g = 0.0010 kg. W<sub>net</sub> = ½mv² = ½(0.0010)(12)² = <strong>0.072 J</strong>.'))
answer(36,p('For constant g, ΔE<sub>p</sub> = mgΔh. The second 5 m rise adds <strong>the same potential-energy increase</strong> as the first. Total gain from the original level doubles.'))
answer(37,p('W = mgh, so m = 620/[(9.81)(2.5)] = 25.28 kg ≈ <strong>25 kg</strong> (25.3 kg before two-significant-figure rounding). Assume no other energy changes.'))
answer(38,p('h = W/(mg) = 240/[(4)(9.81)] = <strong>6.1 m</strong> to the precision implied by the data.'))
answer(39,p('Choose the bottom as zero gravitational potential energy. With negligible losses, mgh = ½mv².','v = √(2gh) = √[(2)(9.81)(0.36)] = 2.658 m/s ≈ <strong>2.7 m/s</strong>. Only vertical rise matters.'))
answer(40,p('The drop is 3.0 m, not the 9 m web length. mgh = ½mv² gives v = √[(2)(9.81)(3.0)] = <strong>7.7 m/s</strong> at the bottom, assuming release from rest and negligible resistance.'))
answer(41,p('At the highest point, ½mv² = mgh. h = 25²/(2 × 9.81) = <strong>31.9 m</strong> before rounding, approximately 32 m vertically. Friction would reduce the stopping height.'))
answer(42,p('h<sub>max</sub> = v²/(2g) = 10²/(2 × 9.81) = <strong>5.1 m</strong>. Mass cancels.','This is the limiting height at which the car stops. To actually cross the crest with nonzero speed, the hill must be lower or additional energy must be supplied.'))
answer(43,p('Assume the skier starts from rest. Two thirds down a straight slope corresponds to a vertical drop of (⅔)(60.0) = 40.0 m.','E<sub>k</sub> = mgΔh = (80.0)(9.81)(40.0) = <strong>3.14 × 10⁴ J</strong>. If there is initial kinetic energy, add it.'))
answer(44,parts('Interpreting the stated 600 m as vertical rise, ΔE<sub>p</sub> = (75)(9.81)(600) = <strong>4.4 × 10⁵ J</strong>. An along-slope distance alone would require the slope angle.','h = ΔE<sub>p</sub>/(mg) = (3.68 × 10⁵)/[(50)(9.81)] ≈ <strong>750 m</strong>. This also agrees with (1500)sin 30° = 750 m.'))
answer(45,p('h = 60 cm = 0.60 m. g = W/(mh) = 0.24/[(0.245)(0.60)] = <strong>1.6 m/s²</strong> (1.63 m/s² before rounding), assuming a slow lift with no other losses.'))
answer(46,parts('After falling halfway, Δh = 1.4 m. v = √(2gΔh) = √[(2)(9.81)(1.4)] = <strong>5.2 m/s downward</strong>.','Just before impact, Δh = 2.8 m, so v = √[(2)(9.81)(2.8)] = <strong>7.4 m/s downward</strong>. Ignore air resistance.'))
answer(47,parts('E<sub>k</sub> = ½(65)(2.5)² = 203.125 J ≈ <strong>2.0 × 10² J</strong>.','The centre of mass can rise by h = v²/(2g) = 2.5²/(2 × 9.81) = <strong>0.32 m</strong> above its initial level, assuming all kinetic energy becomes gravitational potential energy. The initial body height is not specified, so this is a rise, not an absolute foot height.'))
answer(48,parts('Fraction of mechanical energy lost = 1 − h<sub>rebound</sub>/h<sub>drop</sub> = 1 − 1.6/2.0 = 0.20. <strong>20%</strong> is lost from the mechanical stores.','Energy goes into thermal energy, sound, deformation, and motion of the floor and surroundings. Total energy conservation still holds.'))
answer(49,p('The total mass is 12 + 68 = 80 kg. Net work = ½m(v<sub>f</sub>² − v<sub>i</sub>²) = ½(80)(12.7² − 8.2²) = <strong>3762 J ≈ 3.8 kJ</strong>. More applied work would be needed if resistance also did negative work.'))
answer(50,parts('At maximum compression, E<sub>elastic</sub> = E<sub>k,initial</sub> = ½(2.6)(2.0)² = <strong>5.2 J</strong>, neglecting losses.','Average force magnitude = E/d = 5.2/0.12 = <strong>43 N</strong>. A spring’s force changes as it compresses, so this is not its maximum force. The spring does −5.2 J of work on the cart during compression.'))

answer(51,p('Outdoors, surfaces can reach different temperatures because they absorb sunlight differently and exchange energy differently with air, soil, and water. Grass also loses energy through evaporation.','Indoors, tile and other materials may have similar temperatures, but tile often conducts energy away from your feet faster and therefore feels colder. Touch depends on the rate of energy transfer as well as temperature.'))
answer(52,parts('<strong>Temperature</strong> describes thermal state; in the ideal-gas model it measures average translational kinetic energy per particle. <strong>Thermal/internal energy</strong> is microscopic energy stored in all the particles and interactions. <strong>Heat</strong> is energy transferred because of a temperature difference.','A body stores internal energy; heat names energy in transfer, rather than a substance contained inside it.'))
answer(53,parts('T = 20 + 273.15 = <strong>293.15 K ≈ 293 K</strong>.','Double the absolute temperature, not Celsius: T₂ = 2(293.15) = <strong>586.30 K</strong>. In Celsius this is 586.30 − 273.15 = <strong>313.15°C ≈ 313°C</strong>. The textbook’s rounded 273 conversion gives the same whole-number results.'))
answer(54,parts('For the intended large-pot/small-nail comparison, the water has much more total thermal energy because of its large mass. Total internal energy cannot in general be ranked from temperature alone.','The red-hot nail has the higher temperature and therefore higher average translational kinetic energy in the textbook model.','Heat transfers <strong>from the hotter nail to the cooler water</strong>: the nail loses energy and water gains it.','Assuming five minutes is enough to reach thermal equilibrium and other transfers are negligible: (i) the large water sample still has much more total thermal energy; (ii) the temperatures, and the average translational kinetic energies in the textbook model, are equal. Five minutes alone does not prove equilibrium.'))
answer(55,p('Heating at the bottom sets up <strong>convection currents</strong>: warmer, usually less dense water rises, while cooler water descends toward the heater. Bulk fluid motion distributes energy much faster than conduction alone.'))
answer(56,parts('By day, warm air rises over the warmer land. Surface air flows <strong>from sea to land</strong>, with a return flow aloft.','At night, under the stated conditions, warm air rises over the relatively warmer sea. Surface air flows <strong>from land to sea</strong>, with a return flow aloft.','The intended choice is <strong>very early morning</strong>, when an offshore land breeze can help a boat head out. Near evening the circulation may be transitioning; actual winds also depend on larger weather systems.')+breeze())
answer(57,p('The vacuum gap greatly reduces conduction and prevents convection across the gap. Shiny surfaces reduce net radiation exchange. The insulating stopper and narrow neck reduce conduction and fluid exchange; the stopper also reduces evaporation.','The supporting material and stopper still conduct some energy, so insulation slows transfer rather than preventing every loss.'))
answer(58,p('<strong>Dirty snow usually melts faster</strong> in sunlight because a thin dark contamination layer reduces reflectivity and increases absorption of solar radiation. This compares similar snow under otherwise similar conditions.'))
answer(59,p('<strong>Report your observed order</strong> of wax release, for the actual metals and apparatus tested. The source does not supply a metal list or measured release times, so an experimental ranking cannot be invented.','For otherwise similar rods, faster wax melting suggests faster energy conduction. Typical equal-sized copper or aluminum rods conduct well compared with steel, but rod dimensions, contact, and heating conditions must also be controlled.'))
answer(60,p('Examples of good conducting metals used in cookware are <strong>copper, aluminum, and iron</strong>. Copper and aluminum spread energy rapidly; cast iron conducts less well than those two but has other useful cooking properties.'))
answer(61,p('Copper gauze conducts energy away from gases crossing its small openings. If the gas is cooled below its ignition temperature, a flame does not propagate through the gauze even though unburned gas can pass through.','Thus a flame can be established in one region without automatically igniting gas in neighboring regions separated by gauze. This explains the different burning locations in the teacher’s trials; record which regions actually burn. Results depend on gauze temperature and the apparatus.'))
answer(62,p('Use an insulating container with a lid and trapped-air layers around the ice, minimize contact with conducting supports, and shield it from radiant heating.','For a fair test, use equal ice masses and initial temperatures, identical room conditions, and the same criterion for “still solid.” Measure melting time rather than assuming a winning design.'))
answer(63,p('Heating commonly expands a fluid, lowering its density. The less-dense warm fluid rises in surrounding cooler, denser fluid. Cooler fluid descends elsewhere and replaces it, making a circulation.','In the water loop, water rises on the heated side. In the convection box, warm air rises out above the candle, drawing cooler air and smoke down the other chimney.'))
answer(64,p('Heating from below generally makes warm water rise toward the ice, so <strong>convection transfers energy upward and the ice melts</strong>. Heating only the top creates a more stable warm-over-cold arrangement, so the lower ice can remain while the upper water boils.','This is the intended qualitative prediction; water’s density behavior near 4°C can affect detailed low-temperature motion.'))
answer(65,p('<strong>Use your measured temperature changes</strong> to answer which water sample warmed more. Under comparable conditions, the black container is expected to warm more than the foil-covered container. The source table is blank, so no experimental temperatures are known.'))
answer(66,p('The black surface generally absorbs more of the lamp’s incident radiation, while shiny foil reflects more. The absorbed energy increases the water and container internal energies.','Keep water masses, starting temperatures, lamp distances, container dimensions, and exposure times comparable. Heat loss and container heat capacity also affect the result.'))
answer(67,p('Place the radiometer so the lamp cannot shine directly on it, using a screen. Aim a mirror so the reflected lamp radiation reaches the vanes. Compare rotation with the mirror aimed at the radiometer and aimed away, keeping source distance and background conditions as similar as possible.','A reproducible response only when the reflected beam reaches it supports reflection of the radiation. Include a direct-illumination comparison and allow for the radiometer’s response time. It is a qualitative detector, not an automatic measurement of reflected power.'))
answer(68,p('A molecule can have <strong>translational, rotational, and vibrational kinetic energy</strong>, depending on its structure and the available motions.'))
answer(69,p('T = 30 + 273.15 = <strong>303.15 K ≈ 303 K</strong>.'))
answer(70,p('<strong>Radiation</strong>, carried by electromagnetic waves, can transfer energy through a vacuum. Conduction and ordinary convection require matter.'))
answer(71,p('At night in the intended situation, the water is warmer than the land. Air rises over the water, giving flow <strong>from 2 to 1</strong>; cooler air descends over land, <strong>from 3 to 4</strong>. Surface return flow goes from land to sea, and upper return flow goes from sea to land.')+breeze())
answer(72,p('A reflective inner surface reduces net radiative heat loss from the body. The enclosing material can also limit wind and evaporation. The suit conserves energy; it does not supply an independent source of heat.','<strong>Source correction:</strong> the question’s implication that a reflective covering should categorically be withheld from a hypothermic person is misleading. Preventing further heat loss remains useful; insulation alone may not provide adequate rewarming. Hypothermia is an emergency requiring appropriate first aid. See <a href="https://www.redcross.org/get-help/how-to-prepare-for-emergencies/types-of-emergencies/winter-storm/hypothermia-and-frostbite.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">American Red Cross guidance</a>.'))
answer(73,p('The main direct transfer from an open fire to a seated person in front of it is <strong>thermal radiation</strong>. Much of the hot air rises instead of moving straight toward the person.'))
answer(74,p('<strong>Convection</strong> circulates the fluid: warmer soup rises and cooler soup descends. Conduction also acts locally, especially at the pot surface.'))
answer(75,p('Metal skate blades conduct energy away from your skin faster than the boot material does. They can therefore feel colder <strong>even at the same initial temperature</strong>.'))
answer(76,p('A lake contains a much larger mass of water, so a large amount of energy must leave before its bulk temperature changes appreciably. Mixing can bring warmer water to the surface, and freezing also requires removal of latent energy.','A shallow puddle has much less stored energy and a large exposed surface relative to its volume, so it can cool and freeze sooner. Lake water and puddle water do not have different specific heat capacities merely because their sizes differ.'))
answer(77,p('Q = 25 000 J and c = 4200 J/(kg·°C). ΔT = Q/(mc) = 25 000/[(50.0)(4200)] = 0.1190°C.','T<sub>f</sub> = 20.0 + 0.1190 ≈ <strong>20.1°C</strong>.'))
answer(78,p('ΔT = 80 − 18 = 62°C. Q = mcΔT = (90.0)(4200)(62) = <strong>2.3436 × 10⁷ J</strong> before rounding, approximately <strong>23 MJ</strong>.'))
answer(79,p('ΔT = (1.0 × 10⁶)/[(10.0)(4200)] = 23.81°C. T<sub>f</sub> = 15 + 23.81 = <strong>39°C</strong> after rounding.'))
answer(80,p('Q<sub>water</sub> = (12)(4200)(20 − 100) = −4.032 × 10⁶ J. The magnitude released to the environment is <strong>4.0 MJ</strong>.'))
answer(81,p('Water has a <strong>high specific heat capacity</strong>, so a substantial energy transfer causes a relatively small temperature rise. Circulating water can therefore carry considerable energy away from an engine. Real coolant mixtures also provide other useful properties.'))
answer(82,p('ΔT = 26.2 − 20.0 = 6.2°C. c = Q/(mΔT) = 1200/[(0.500)(6.2)] = 387.1 J/(kg·°C) ≈ <strong>3.9 × 10² J/(kg·°C)</strong>.'))
answer(83,p('Useful work = (1200 N)(1.3 m) = 1560 J. Input work = (820 N)(2.4 m) = 1968 J.','η = 1560/1968 = 0.7927 ≈ <strong>79%</strong>. Use vertical height for gravitational energy and ramp length for effort work, assuming no change in speed.'))
answer(84,p('Useful output = (150)(3.0) = 450 J. η = 450/500 = 0.90, so efficiency is <strong>90%</strong>.'))
answer(85,p('P<sub>water</sub> = ηP<sub>in</sub> = (0.80)(1200) = <strong>960 W</strong>.'))
answer(86,p('P<sub>light</sub> = (0.050)(60) = <strong>3.0 W</strong>. The remaining approximately 57 W is mainly transferred into thermal energy.'))
answer(87,p('η = E<sub>useful</sub>/E<sub>in</sub> = 120 kJ/1000 kJ = 0.12 = <strong>12%</strong>.','The other 880 kJ per input megajoule can heat the engine, coolant, exhaust, tires, road, and surroundings, with smaller sound and other transfers. It is not destroyed.'))
answer(88,p('<strong>Use your temperature-time graph.</strong> P<sub>water</sub> = mc × slope = (0.300)(4200)(ΔT/Δt) = <strong>1260 × slope</strong> in watts when slope is in °C/s.','No measurements are supplied. For example only, a slope of 0.100°C/s would give 126 W; it is not an experimental result from the source.'))
answer(89,p('Using the source’s formula, relative discrepancy = <strong>100(P<sub>rated</sub> − P<sub>water</sub>)/P<sub>rated</sub></strong>. Substitute your measured water-heating power and the actual label rating.','The source calls this “percent difference,” but it is a discrepancy relative to the rating rather than the symmetric percent-difference convention. Values above the rating should trigger a review of measurements and assumptions, rather than being silently forced positive.'))
answer(90,p('η = <strong>P<sub>water</sub>/P<sub>rated</sub> × 100%</strong>. Both quantities must refer to comparable operating conditions. Your measured slope and the label rating are needed for a numerical value.','This describes the useful fraction heating the water, rather than a failure of energy conservation.'))
answer(91,p('Some energy heats the hot plate, beaker, thermometer, and surrounding air, and some is transferred by radiation and evaporation. Therefore useful water-heating power is less than input power.','Thermometer lag, changing heat loss, imperfect mixing, and differences between label and actual operating power also affect the experimental result.'))
answer(92,p('Use c<sub>ice</sub> = 2100 J/(kg·°C): Q = (1.6)(2100)[0 − (−15)] = 50 400 J ≈ <strong>5.0 × 10⁴ J</strong>.','This warms the ice to its melting point but does not melt it; melting would require a separate latent-energy term.'))
answer(93,p('With c<sub>lead</sub> = 130 J/(kg·°C), Q<sub>lead</sub> = (5.0)(130)(20 − 250) = −149 500 J. It releases <strong>1.5 × 10⁵ J</strong> of energy.'))
answer(94,p('Q = (5.0)(4200)(97 − 20) = 1 617 000 J ≈ <strong>1.6 MJ</strong>.'))
answer(95,p('Q = (50.0)(4200)(85 − 15) = <strong>14 700 000 J = 14.7 MJ</strong> before any further rounding.'))
answer(96,p('c = Q/(mΔT) = 24 000/[(0.600)(220 − 20)] = <strong>200 J/(kg·°C)</strong>. The source’s “20°” is interpreted as 20°C.'))
answer(97,parts('Read the plotted line using widely separated points, approximately (0 s, 20°C) and (1000 s, 60°C). Slope ≈ (60 − 20)/1000 = <strong>0.040°C/s</strong>. The horizontal-axis numbers are hundreds of seconds.','P<sub>water</sub> ≈ (0.500)(4200)(0.040) = <strong>84 W</strong>. Graph readings are approximate.','A nominal rating of about <strong>100 W</strong> is plausible for a standard incandescent bulb delivering roughly this much heat, but the graph directly determines only useful water-heating power. Exact electrical rating also requires the fraction of input energy absorbed by the water.')+p('<strong>Answer-key discrepancy:</strong> the printed key gives 0.0455°C/s and 95.6 W. Those two numbers agree with each other, but the displayed line is closer to 0.040°C/s. Use the plotted data and show the points you read.'))
answer(98,p('Large nearby water masses have a high total heat capacity and exchange energy slowly, moderating coastal air temperatures. Water circulation and evaporation also redistribute energy. Land typically warms and cools more rapidly, allowing larger inland temperature swings.'))
answer(99,p('Incandescent light output = (0.05)(60) = 3 W. Fluorescent light output = (0.15)(60) = 9 W.','For the same duration, the fluorescent lamp emits <strong>three times as much useful light energy</strong>, or 6t J more over t seconds. “Three times as much” means 200% more, not 300% more.'))
answer(100,p('Useful energy = (1.00)(4200)(88 − 18) = 294 000 J. Input time = 3.6 × 60 = 216 s; input energy = (1500)(216) = 324 000 J.','η = 294 000/324 000 = 0.9074 ≈ <strong>91%</strong>.'))

answer(101,parts('W = mgh = (0.67)(9.81)(1.5) = 9.859 J ≈ <strong>9.9 J</strong> of work against gravity.','For motion only 1.5 m sideways at the same shelf height, with no speed change, the vertical support force does <strong>0 J</strong> of work. If the wording is read as also lifting the book from the table again, that separate 1.5 m rise still requires 9.9 J; the sideways part adds no gravitational work.'))
answer(102,p('N = 75 N, so friction = (0.40)(75) = 30 N. At steady speed the horizontal push also has magnitude 30 N.','W<sub>push</sub> = Fd = (30)(4.2) = <strong>126 J ≈ 1.3 × 10² J</strong>. Friction does −126 J; the net work on the block is zero.'))
answer(103,p('The ramp can reduce the effort force, making the task easier, but the effort acts over a greater distance. For the same vertical rise and no speed change, useful work remains <strong>mgh</strong>.','An ideal ramp saves no work; friction usually increases input work. The everyday claim uses “work” to mean effort or difficulty rather than the physics quantity.'))
answer(104,parts('The moving load block is supported by four equal-tension rope segments: <strong>IMA = 4</strong>.','F<sub>e</sub> = 240/4 = <strong>60 N</strong>.','Effort distance = IMA × load distance = (4)(2.8) = <strong>11.2 m</strong>, or about 11 m to two significant figures.','P<sub>out</sub> = F<sub>L</sub>h/t = (240)(2.8)/1.6 = <strong>420 W</strong>. Ideal input power is the same.'))
answer(105,parts('MA = F<sub>L</sub>/F<sub>e</sub> = 840/70.0 = <strong>12</strong>.','For an ideal system with IMA = 12, d<sub>e</sub> = (12)(0.200) = <strong>2.40 m</strong>. The given force ratio is actual MA; if friction is significant it does not alone determine the distance ratio.','Work on the engine = (840)(0.200) = <strong>168 J</strong>.','Minimum input work = useful output work = <strong>168 J</strong>, neglecting losses. A real system requires at least this much.'))
answer(106,p('1 kW·h = (1000 J/s)(3600 s) = <strong>3.6 × 10⁶ J</strong>. A kilowatt-hour is energy, not power. The J/s unit printed in the source answer key is incorrect here.'))
answer(107,p('Assume release from rest and a constant-angle slope. One third of the height has been lost, so one third of the initial potential energy has become kinetic energy: E<sub>k</sub> = (⅓)(60 kJ) = <strong>20 kJ</strong>. The remaining potential energy is 40 kJ.'))
answer(108,p('Read “lightens … by ⅓” literally: the new clubhead mass is (⅔)m. The speed is 3v. Its kinetic-energy ratio is [(⅔)m(3v)²]/(mv²) = (⅔)(9) = <strong>6</strong>.','If the same fraction transfers to the ball, the new transferred energy is <strong>6E<sub>k</sub></strong>. If the intended wording were “reduces the mass <em>to</em> ⅓,” it would instead be 3E<sub>k</sub>, the printed key’s value. The actual energy transfer also depends on the collision.'))
answer(109,p('At the highest point the kinetic energy has become gravitational potential energy. h = v²/(2g) = 1.8²/(2 × 9.81) = 0.1651 m ≈ <strong>0.17 m</strong> above the lowest point.'))
answer(110,p('After the string catches the rod, the small-loop radius is r = 10 cm = 0.10 m. Merely reaching the top requires a rise of 2r, but that would leave zero speed and the string could not remain taut.','At the top, mg + T = mv²/r. The limiting case T = 0 requires v² = gr. Conservation from release at height h above the loop bottom gives mgh = mg(2r) + ½m(gr).','Thus <strong>h<sub>min</sub> = 2.5r = 0.25 m = 25 cm above the bottom</strong>, or 15 cm above the rod. Strictly positive top tension requires a slightly larger height. The source key’s 20 cm uses height alone and omits the required top kinetic energy.'))
answer(111,p('Only the vertical drop 5.0 m enters the energy calculation, not the 12 m vine length. v = √(2gΔh) = √[(2)(9.81)(5.0)] = <strong>9.9 m/s</strong>, assuming release from rest and negligible losses.'))
answer(112,p('T = 250 + 273.15 = <strong>523.15 K ≈ 523 K</strong>.'))
answer(113,p('Initial absolute temperature = 25 + 273.15 = 298.15 K. Double this to 596.30 K, then convert back: 596.30 − 273.15 = <strong>323.15°C ≈ 323°C</strong>. The required temperature increase is 298.15°C.'))
answer(114,p('Metals contain mobile electrons that transfer energy rapidly through the material, in addition to energy transfer through lattice vibrations. These microscopic mechanisms make metals good thermal conductors.'))
answer(115,parts('In a forced-air furnace system, moving warm air transfers energy by <strong>convection</strong>. Hot-water systems transport energy in circulating water and then transfer it into rooms by convection and radiation.','Heat passes through solid wall materials mainly by <strong>conduction</strong>; air leakage and convection in gaps can contribute.','Energy reaches and passes through the roof by conduction; the exterior then loses energy to air by <strong>convection</strong> and to the surroundings by <strong>radiation</strong>. Rising warm air inside and leakage can also matter.'))
answer(116,p('Warmer water is generally less dense than cooler water at normal hot-water-tank temperatures, so it rises while cooler water sinks. This convection and buoyant layering leave warmer water near the top.'))
answer(117,p('Both samples cool by 80°C. Water releases (1)(4200)(80) = <strong>336 000 J</strong>; metal releases (1)(420)(80) = <strong>33 600 J</strong>.','Water releases <strong>10 times as much</strong>, or <strong>302 400 J more</strong>. Their signed energy changes are negative; these are released magnitudes.'))
answer(118,p('t = 5.00 × 60 = 300 s. Q = Pt = (1500)(300) = 450 000 J.','ΔT = Q/(mc) = 450 000/[(10.0)(4200)] = 10.714°C, so T<sub>f</sub> ≈ <strong>36°C</strong> (35.714°C before rounding).'))
answer(119,p('Q = Pt = (1200)(10.0 × 60) = <strong>720 000 J = 0.720 MJ</strong>, given 100% useful efficiency.'))
answer(120,p('η = P<sub>water</sub>/P<sub>in</sub> = 1400/1500 = 0.9333…, so the efficiency is <strong>93%</strong>, approximately 93.3% before rounding.'))

answer(121,p('ΔE<sub>p</sub> = mgh = (45)(9.81)(6.0) = 2648.7 J ≈ <strong>2.6 × 10³ J</strong>.'))
answer(122,parts('For a changing spring force, use the stated average: E<sub>elastic</sub> = F<sub>avg</sub>x = (1.2)(0.030) = <strong>0.036 J</strong>. Do not multiply this average-force result by another ½.','With m = 10.0 g = 0.0100 kg, ½mv² = 0.036, so v = √[(2)(0.036)/0.0100] = <strong>2.7 m/s</strong>.'))
answer(123,p('v = 90/3.6 = 25 m/s. E<sub>k</sub> = ½(1.0 × 10³)(25)² = 312 500 J ≈ <strong>3.1 × 10⁵ J</strong>.'))
answer(124,p('E<sub>p</sub> = mgh = (75)(9.81)(2.0 × 10³) = 1.4715 × 10⁶ J ≈ <strong>1.5 MJ</strong>, relative to the hill’s bottom.'))
answer(125,p('In the pictured arrangement, the pulley is attached to the 60 kg student and the other rope end to the 40 kg student. With ideal rope tension T, the 60 kg side receives <strong>2T</strong>, while the 40 kg side receives <strong>T</strong>.','With similar chair-floor friction coefficients, the 60 kg student should start moving first: that side needs T ≈ μ(60g)/2 = μ30g to overcome static friction, compared with μ40g on the other side. With negligible friction both move toward each other, and the 60 kg side accelerates more: 2T/60 > T/40.','The prediction depends on friction and chair masses; test it. Comparing body masses alone misses the pulley’s unequal forces. The external person pulling the free end means the two students alone are not an isolated system.'))
answer(126,parts('System A has two moving pulleys plus a rope end anchored to the moving block, giving five load-supporting tensions: <strong>IMA = 5</strong>.','Effort = 600.0/5 = <strong>120.0 N</strong>.','Work on the load = (600.0)(1.2) = <strong>720 J</strong>.','Effort distance = (5)(1.2) = <strong>6.0 m</strong>.','Ideal work by you = (120.0)(6.0) = <strong>720 J</strong>. The 710 J printed elsewhere in the source key is inconsistent with these data.'))
answer(127,parts('System B also has five supporting tensions: <strong>IMA = 5</strong>. The additional fixed pulley redirects the free end; it does not add another supporting strand to the moving block.','h = d<sub>e</sub>/IMA = 3.5/5 = <strong>0.70 m</strong>.'))
answer(128,parts('System C consists of <strong>four successive movable-pulley stages</strong>. Their ideal advantages multiply: IMA = 2⁴ = <strong>16</strong>.','Load force = IMA × effort = (16)(300) = <strong>4800 N</strong>. Ignore rope and pulley masses and friction.'))
answer(129,p('Useful power = mgh/t = (75)(9.81)(3.0)/2.5 = 882.9 W. In horsepower, 882.9/746 = <strong>1.2 hp</strong> after rounding. This is external mechanical output, not metabolic input.'))
answer(130,p('Use the given approximate rating 3 kW and time 0.5 h = 1800 s: W = Pt = (3000)(1800) = <strong>5.4 × 10⁶ J = 5.4 MJ</strong>. Using 4.0 hp × 746 W/hp gives 5.37 MJ, which rounds to the same result.'))
answer(131,p('Q = Pt = (1500)(60) = <strong>90 000 J = 90 kJ</strong>, assuming the heater’s stated power is the heating output.'))
answer(132,p('Each stated swing retains 99.0% of the preceding mechanical energy. At a turning point E = mgh, so after four such swings: h₄ = h₀(0.99)⁴ = (0.3000)(0.99)⁴ = <strong>0.2882 m</strong>.','This treats “swing” as the energy-loss interval defined by the question. Do not simply subtract 4% of the initial height; the losses compound.'))
answer(133,p('v = 285/3.6 = 79.1667 m/s. From mgh = ½mv², h = v²/(2g) = (79.1667)²/(2 × 9.81) = <strong>319 m</strong>. This ideal result ignores air resistance, as requested.'))
answer(134,parts('Fireplace to a person in front: mainly <strong>radiation</strong>.','Hot gases moving up the chimney: <strong>convection</strong>.','Stove element into a contacting pan: mainly <strong>conduction</strong>; radiation can also contribute across a gap.','Energy distributed through the soup: mainly <strong>convection</strong>.','A forced-air furnace distributes energy with moving air: <strong>convection</strong>. Conduction and radiation occur at solid surfaces and room boundaries.'))
answer(135,p('Under ordinary illumination, the vanes turn with the <strong>black faces trailing</strong>, as if those faces were being pushed backward. They absorb more radiation and become warmer than the shiny faces.','The motion comes from temperature-gradient-driven forces in the rarefied residual gas, involving the vane edges. It is not adequately explained by light pressure or simply by greater static pressure on an entire hot face. See <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/physics/0402011" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scandurra, Enhanced radiometric forces</a> for the gas-thermal mechanism.'))
answer(136,p('Snow traps air and is a poor conductor, so the walls slow energy loss from the relatively warm interior. The shelter also limits wind-driven exchange. Heat from occupants and other sources can keep the interior warmer than outdoors.','The igloo does not create energy, and “warm” means warmer than its surroundings rather than necessarily room temperature.'))
answer(137,p('<strong>Specific heat capacity</strong> is the energy required per kilogram of a substance for a one-degree Celsius (or one-kelvin) temperature increase, without a phase change.','c = Q/(mΔT), with units <strong>J/(kg·°C)</strong> or J/(kg·K).'))
answer(138,p('T = 373 − 273.15 = <strong>99.85°C ≈ 100°C</strong>. Using the textbook’s rounded 273 gives 100°C directly.'))
answer(139,p('The metal doorknob conducts energy away from your warmer hand faster than wood does. Therefore it feels colder even when both objects have the same initial temperature.'))
answer(140,p('Q = Pt = (1500)(2.00 × 60) = 180 000 J. ΔT = Q/(mc) = 180 000/[(5.00)(4200)] = 8.571°C.','T<sub>f</sub> = 10 + 8.571 = <strong>18.6°C</strong> before rounding to the precision of the initial temperature, approximately 19°C if 10°C is specified only to a whole degree. The 1500 W is already power delivered to the water.'))
answer(141,p('Q<sub>water</sub> = (1.30)(4200)(99.0 − 25.0) = 404 040 J. E<sub>in</sub> = (1500)(5.00 × 60) = 450 000 J.','η = 404 040/450 000 = 0.8979 ≈ <strong>90%</strong> (89.8% before rounding).'))
answer(142,p('Heat output is the non-light fraction in this model: P<sub>heat</sub> = (1 − 0.15)(40) = 34 W. In 1 s, Q = Pt = <strong>34 J</strong>.'))
answer(143,p('m = 800.0 g = 0.8000 kg; ΔT = 99 − 20 = 79°C. Q<sub>water</sub> = (0.8000)(4200)(79) = 265 440 J.','Input energy = (1200)(4.0 × 60) = 288 000 J. Efficiency = 265 440/288 000 × 100% ≈ <strong>92%</strong>.'))
answer(144,p('η = P<sub>water</sub>/P<sub>input</sub> = 500/600 = 0.8333…, so the percolator is <strong>83% efficient</strong>. The source calls it a kettle in the last sentence; the same stated device is intended.'))

assert set(ANSWERS) == {f'q-{n}' for n in range(1,145)}
